<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1921 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">İstiklal Marşı</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="tr">İstiklâl Marşı</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of Turkey, a poem chosen in 1921, during the war for independence, from 724 that were sent in.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Mehmet Âkif Ersoy</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Osman Zeki Üngör (an earlier setting by Çağatay; harmonised and orchestrated by Edgar Manas)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Turkish National Movement; the Republic of Turkey</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Anatolia, present-day Turkey</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>adopted by the Grand National Assembly on 12 March 1921; music adopted 1930</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Mehmet Âkif Ersoy, published in 1921; music by Osman Zeki Üngör, adopted 1930)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istiklal-mars">İstiklal Marşı</a>&quot; (&quot;Independence March&quot;) was chosen in a nationwide competition organised in 1921, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> had been fully dissolved, by the Turkish National Movement, the forces led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a> that were fighting both invading foreign powers and the Ottoman court, which the movement held to be treasonous for its part in the partition of the homeland after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sèvres</a> of 1920. The competition was to find a National March that would motivate the fighters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a> and give pride to the new homeland that victory would bring. A total of 724 poems were submitted. Mehmet Âkif Ersoy, a well-known poet of the time, at first refused to take part because a money prize was offered, but the National Parliament persuaded him to send a poem and set the reward aside. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was dedicated Kahraman Ordumuza, &quot;To Our Heroic Army&quot;.</p><p>Ersoy's ten-stanza poem was recited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> by the deputy Hamdullah Suphi on 1 March 1921 and, after a parliamentary committee had examined it, the deputies adopted it unanimously; it was officially adopted on 12 March 1921, two and a half years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> was proclaimed on 29 October 1923. The prize went to a society of veterans. An early setting by Çağatay was used for only six years. In 1930 Parliament adopted a new composition by Osman Zeki Üngör, a virtuoso composer and the first conductor of the Presidential Symphony Orchestra, and in 1932 the government commissioned the musician Edgar Manas to harmonise and orchestrate Üngör's melody, which gave the anthem its final, official form.</p><p>The full text has 41 lines in ten stanzas, but only the first two quatrains are sung at official ceremonies. The anthem is heard at state and military events, national festivals and bayrams, sporting events and school ceremonies. A framed copy usually hangs above the blackboard in Turkish classrooms, beside the flag, a photograph of Atatürk and his address to the nation's youth from the speech he ended on 20 October 1927, and the first two quatrains were printed on the back of the Turkish 100 lira banknotes of 1983–1989. In 1983 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Turkish Republic</a> of Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> also adopted the anthem, under Article II of its constitution.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A poem of love for the homeland, of freedom and of faith, written for an army at war. It speaks to the flag, to the soil and to the human spirit, praising hope, devotion and sacrifice in the cause of liberty and remembering the soldiers who died for it. The mood is fervent and resolute.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Turkish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Turkish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Korkma, sönmez bu şafaklarda yüzen al sancak;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fear not! For the crimson flag that proudly ripples in this glorious twilight, shall not fade,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Sönmeden yurdumun üstünde tüten en son ocak.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Before the last fiery hearth that is ablaze within my nation is extinguished.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">O benim milletimin yıldızıdır, parlayacak;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For That is the star of my nation, and it will forever shine;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">O benimdir, o benim milletimindir ancak.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is mine; and solely belongs to my valiant nation.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Çatma, kurban olayım, çehreni ey nazlı hilâl</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Frown not, I beseech you, oh thou coy crescent,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kahraman ırkıma bir gül! Ne bu şiddet, bu celâl?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But smile upon my heroic race! Why the anger, why the rage?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Sana olmaz dökülen kanlarımız sonra helâl..</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This blood of ours which we shed for you shall not be blessed otherwise;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Hakkıdır, Hakk'a tapan milletimin istiklâl.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For Freedom is the absolute right of my God-worshiping nation.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Ben ezelden beridir hür yaşadım, hür yaşarım</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I have been free since the beginning and forever shall be so.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Hangi çılgın bana zincir uracakmış? Şaşarım!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What madman shall put me in chains! I defy the very idea!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kükremiş sel gibiyim; bendimi çiğner aşarım;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I'm like the roaring flood; powerful and independent,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Yırtarım dağları, enginlere sığmam, taşarım.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I'll tear apart mountains, exceed the heavens and still gush out!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Garbın âfâkını sarmışsa çelik zırhlı duvar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The lands of the West may be surrounded with walls of steel,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Benim iman dolu göğsüm gibi serhaddim var.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But I have borders guarded by the mighty chest of a believer.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Ulusun, korkma! Nasıl böyle bir imanı boğar,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Recognize your innate strength, my friend! And think: how can this fiery faith ever be killed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">&quot;Medeniyet&quot; dediğin tek dişi kalmış canavar?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By that battered, single-fanged monster you call &quot;civilization&quot;?</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 41 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Arkadaş yurduma alçakları uğratma, sakın</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My friend! Leave not my homeland to the hands of villainous men!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Siper et gövdeni, dursun bu hayasızca akın.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Render your chest as armor and your body as trench! Stop this disgraceful rush!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Doğacaktır sana vadettiği günler Hakk'ın..</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For soon shall come the joyous days of divine promise...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kim bilir belki, yarın; belki yarından da yakın.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who knows? Perhaps tomorrow? Perhaps even sooner!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Bastığın yerleri &quot;toprak?&quot; diyerek geçme, tanı!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>View not the soil you tread on as mere earth - recognize it!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Düşün altındaki binlerce kefensiz yatanı,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And think about the shroudless thousands who lie so nobly beneath you.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Sen şehid oğlusun. İncitme, yazıktır, atanı;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You're the noble son of a martyr, take shame, hurt not your ancestor!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Verme dünyaları alsan da bu cennet vatanı.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Unhand not, even when you're promised worlds, this paradise of a homeland.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Kim bu cennet vatanın uğruna olmaz ki feda?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What man would not die for this heavenly piece of land?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Şühedâ fışkıracak toprağı sıksan şühedâ!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Martyrs would gush out should one simply squeeze the soil! Martyrs!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Canı, cananı, bütün varımı alsın da Hudâ,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May God take all my loved ones and possessions from me if He will,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Etmesin tek vatanımdan beni dünyada cüdâ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But may He not deprive me of my one true homeland for the world.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Ruhumun senden, İlâhi, şudur ancak emeli:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Oh glorious God, the sole wish of my pain-stricken heart is that,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Değmesin mâbedimin göğsüne nâmahrem eli.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>No heathen's hand should ever touch the bosom of my sacred Temples.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Bu ezanlar -ki şahâdetleri dinin temeli-</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>These adhans, whose shahadahs are the foundations of my religion,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Ebedî yurdumun üstünde benim inlemeli.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May their noble sound last loud and wide over my eternal homeland.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">O zaman vecd ile bin secde eder -varsa- taşım,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For only then, shall my fatigued tombstone, if there is one, prostrate a thousand times in ecstasy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Her cerihamdan İlâhi, boşanıp kanlı yaşım,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And tears of fiery blood shall flow out of my every wound,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Fışkırır ruh-ı mücerred gibi yerden naşım;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And my lifeless body shall gush out from the earth like an eternal spirit,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">O zaman yükselerek arşa değer belki başım.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Perhaps only then, shall I peacefully ascend and at long last reach the heavens.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Dalgalan sen de şafaklar gibi ey şanlı hilâl!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So ripple and wave like the bright dawning sky, oh thou glorious crescent,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Olsun artık dökülen kanlarımın hepsi helâl.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So that our every last drop of blood may finally be blessed and worthy!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Ebediyyen sana yok, ırkıma yok izmihlâl:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Neither you nor my race shall ever be extinguished!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Hakkıdır hür yaşamış bayrağımın hürriyet;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For freedom is the absolute right of my ever-free flag;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="tr">Hakkıdır Hakk'a tapan milletimin istiklâl.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For freedom is the absolute right of my God-worshiping nation!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Mehmet Âkif Ersoy, as published in the Cerîde-i Resmiye, 21 March 1921. Text from <a href="https://tr.wikisource.org/wiki/%C4%B0stikl%C3%A2l_Mar%C5%9F%C4%B1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: Wikisource translation (GFDL). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Translation%3A%C4%B0stikl%C3%A2l_Mar%C5%9F%C4%B1_(1)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
